# Starr Research Worker — Environment Variables
# Copy this to .env on the deployment host (Hetzner in Phase A; DigitalOcean
# droplet during the migration overlap).

# ── Authentication ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Bearer token clients use to call this worker.
WORKER_API_KEY=your-secret-worker-api-key

# Anthropic API key for Claude AI extraction + Vision OCR.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-your-anthropic-key

# Claude model pin (optional). Defaults to claude-sonnet-4-6.
# RESEARCH_AI_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-6

# ── Supabase ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-supabase-service-role-key

# ── Runtime ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NODE_ENV=production
PORT=3100

# ── Browser backend ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Selects how worker/src/lib/browser-factory.ts acquires Chromium.
#   local        Run Playwright locally (development; default outside CI)
#   browserbase  Connect to a Browserbase CDP session (Phase A; needs keys)
#   stub         Return a fake browser whose methods throw (CI / unit tests)
BROWSER_BACKEND=local
PLAYWRIGHT_HEADLESS=true
# Absolute path to a Chromium binary. Optional: leave empty and Playwright resolves its own.
# Set it when the host has full Chromium but not the separate headless-shell download — Playwright
# prefers the shell and fails with "Executable doesn't exist" next to a perfectly good browser.
PLAYWRIGHT_CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE=

# Browserbase credentials (Phase A — leave blank in stub mode).
# Sign up at https://www.browserbase.com → Project → API Keys.
# BROWSERBASE_API_KEY=
# BROWSERBASE_PROJECT_ID=
#
# Per-adapter routing for the browserbase backend. Comma-separated list
# of adapter ids (filename stems, e.g. "bell-clerk,fidlar-clerk"). When
# empty, NO adapter is routed to Browserbase even if BROWSER_BACKEND=browserbase.
# Unknown ids in the list are warned-and-ignored at startup.
# BROWSERBASE_ENABLED_ADAPTERS=

# ── Document capture concurrency (E5d) ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# How many documents to capture at once, within one run. Default 3, and the
# code CLAMPS to 4 — this cannot be raised past that by setting the variable.
#
# The limit is politeness, not hardware. capacity.ts caps concurrent runs
# because these are small government servers, and the fastest way to lose
# access to a county portal is to look like a load test. That applies inside a
# run too: a run that gets the firm banned from Bell County is not a faster run.
#
# Set it to 1 to restore the old strictly-sequential behaviour if a county
# starts rate-limiting.
# RESEARCH_CAPTURE_CONCURRENCY=3

# ── CAPTCHA solver ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Selects worker/src/lib/captcha-solver.ts provider.
#   stub       Deterministic fake solver for development + CI (default)
#   capsolver  Real CapSolver provider (Phase A — needs CAPSOLVER_API_KEY)
CAPTCHA_PROVIDER=stub

# Stub solver mode — controls how the stub responds, useful for testing
# escalation paths. Ignored when CAPTCHA_PROVIDER=capsolver.
#   success  Return a fake token (default)
#   fail     Throw provider_error
#   manual   Throw manual_handoff_required
#   timeout  Throw timeout
# CAPTCHA_STUB_MODE=success

# CapSolver credentials (Phase A — leave blank in stub mode).
# Sign up at https://capsolver.com → Dashboard → API Key.
# CAPSOLVER_API_KEY=

# ── Object storage (Cloudflare R2) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Provider-agnostic abstraction in worker/src/lib/storage.ts. New code
# (Phase A integrations) writes through this layer; existing
# artifact-uploader.ts call sites remain on Supabase Storage until a
# future migration PR (see docs/planning/in-progress/PHASE_A_INTEGRATION_PREP.md).
#   local  Write to ./storage/ on the worker disk (default; dev + CI)
#   r2     Write to Cloudflare R2 via the S3 API
STORAGE_BACKEND=local
# STORAGE_LOCAL_ROOT=./storage

# R2 credentials (only consumed when STORAGE_BACKEND=r2).
# Endpoint is https://<R2_ACCOUNT_ID>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com.
# R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
# R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# R2_BUCKET=starr-recon-artifacts
# Note: legacy R2_BUCKET_DOCUMENTS / R2_BUCKET_REPORTS / R2_PUBLIC_BASE
# from earlier sketches were removed — storage.ts only consumes R2_BUCKET.
# When artifact-uploader.ts is migrated to storage.ts (separate future PR),
# any additional bucket vars are introduced there.

# ── Redis (BullMQ + WebSocket pub/sub) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Used by BullMQ for job queueing and by the WebSocket server (server/ws.ts)
# for fanning research-progress events from the worker out to clients.
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# ── WebSocket research-progress channel ─────────────────────────────────────
# Worker emits research events (job started, document discovered, captcha
# resolved, etc.) into Redis; server/ws.ts subscribes and fans them out
# to authorized browser clients. See docs/platform/WEBSOCKET_ARCHITECTURE.md.
# WS_TICKET_SECRET is the HMAC secret for short-lived (60s) connection
# tickets. Deliberately distinct from NEXTAUTH_SECRET so WS auth can be
# rotated independently. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# WS_TICKET_SECRET=

# ── Closure tolerance overrides (optional, per-deploy) ──────────────────────
# Override the defaults in worker/src/lib/closure-tolerance.ts. Counties
# may further override per-county via county-config-registry. See
# docs/CLOSURE_TOLERANCE.md.
# CLOSURE_EXCELLENT_RATIO=10000
# CLOSURE_ACCEPTABLE_RATIO=5000
# CLOSURE_MARGINAL_RATIO=2500

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Added 2026-08-02 (research plan R7). Everything below was read by the code
# and documented nowhere — 47 of the worker's 70 environment variables,
# including every paid-platform credential. A test now fails when the code
# starts reading a variable this file does not mention, because the failure
# mode of an undocumented setting is a deploy that looks fine and behaves
# differently.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Capacity ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How many research runs this box holds at once. LEAVE UNSET unless you have a
# reason: it is computed from cores and RAM at boot (src/infra/capacity.ts) and
# reported on /healthz. Pinning it high is how a 25-minute run dies at minute
# 22 — after the paid documents have already been bought.
# WORKER_MAX_CONCURRENT_PIPELINES=

# ── Deploy identity ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Stamped into /healthz so a running container can be matched to a commit.
# docker compose passes it as a build arg:
#   BUILD_SHA=$(git rev-parse --short HEAD) docker compose up -d --build
# BUILD_SHA=unknown
# GIT_SHA=

# ── Paid document platforms (plan R13) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Each unlocks one purchase adapter. The registry tries free sources first and
# orders paid ones cost-ascending, so an unset vendor is skipped rather than
# failing a run. DEFAULT_PURCHASE_BUDGET caps per-run document spend in USD.
# DEFAULT_PURCHASE_BUDGET=
# TYLER_PAY_USERNAME=
# TYLER_PAY_PASSWORD=
# HENSCHEN_PAY_USERNAME=
# HENSCHEN_PAY_PASSWORD=
# IDOCKET_PAY_USERNAME=
# IDOCKET_PAY_PASSWORD=
# FIDLAR_PAY_USERNAME=
# FIDLAR_PAY_PASSWORD=
# KOFILE_USERNAME=
# KOFILE_PASSWORD=
# KOFILE_PAYMENT_ON_FILE=
# TEXASFILE_USERNAME=
# TEXASFILE_PASSWORD=
# CSLEXI_USERNAME=
# CSLEXI_PASSWORD=
# LANDEX_API_KEY=
# LANDEX_ACCOUNT_ID=
# GOVOS_ACCOUNT_USERNAME=
# GOVOS_ACCOUNT_PASSWORD=
# GOVOS_CREDIT_CARD_TOKEN=

# ── Public data sources + imagery (plan R16) ────────────────────────────────
# Satellite + Street View capture. Check the terms before storing tiles in a
# customer packet — that is an owner decision, not a setting (plan §4.2).
#
# THIS MUST BE A SERVER KEY WITH NO REFERRER RESTRICTION. The worker calls
# Google server-to-server, which sends no Referer header, so a browser key is
# refused with "API keys with referer restrictions cannot be used with this
# API" — however many APIs are enabled on the project. Measured 2026-08-30:
# that is why 22 stored aerial/topo images in production are broken.
#
# GOOGLE_MAPS_SERVER_KEY is preferred; GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY is accepted because
# the key already existed under that name. Either works; set one.
# GOOGLE_MAPS_SERVER_KEY=
# GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY is the BROWSER key. It is listed for
# completeness and is deliberately NOT used as a fallback by worker code: it is
# referrer-restricted and public to every visitor, so using it server-side both
# fails and puts a billed API behind a value anyone can read from the page.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=
# TNRIS_API_KEY=
# USPS_USER_ID=
# TDA_ROW_BASE_URL=

# ── Models ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Overrides for the default routing. Leave unset: plan R6 replaces per-call-site
# model ids with one cheap-first router, and these become the escape hatch
# rather than the mechanism.
# STARR_FIELD_VISION_MODEL=
# STARR_FIELD_WHISPER_MODEL=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# ── Notifications ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# NOTIFICATION_FROM_EMAIL=
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# TWILIO_FROM_NUMBER=

# ── Branding on generated reports ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Per audit item 8h the firm is data, not a constant — these are the worker's
# copy of that identity for PDFs it renders without calling the app.
# COMPANY_NAME=
# COMPANY_ADDRESS=
# COMPANY_RPLS=
# COMPANY_LOGO_PATH=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=

# ── Tuning and scratch paths ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CLERK_RATE_LIMIT_MS pauses between clerk-portal requests — politeness, not
# performance (plan R12).
# CLERK_RATE_LIMIT_MS=
# DEFAULT_DPI=
# PIPELINE_VERSION=
# ANALYSIS_DIR=
# ANALYTICS_DIR=
# BATCH_DIR=

# ── Run budget + timebox (plan R5) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Ceilings for ONE research run. When any is reached the run finishes cleanly
# with what it has and records what it skipped — it does not fail, and it does
# not discard documents it already paid for. A caller may request a shorter
# time via maxResearchTimeMinutes; requests are clamped to 60 minutes so one
# run cannot hold a concurrency slot all afternoon.
# RUN_MAX_MINUTES=25
# RUN_MAX_COST_USD=2.00
# RUN_MAX_PAID_PAGES=20

# ── The research queue poller (plans R28/R29) ───────────────────────────────
# The queue, the atomic claim and the admission limits have existed since R28/R29;
# nothing pulled from them until the poller was wired at boot.
#
# OFF unless RESEARCH_QUEUE_POLLER=1. This is the only loop in the worker that
# spends money and touches other people's servers with no human in the loop —
# each tick can start a 20-30 minute run that logs into a county clerk portal and
# may buy pages. Enabling it is a deployment decision, not a code change.
#
# The poller also refuses to start when the flag is on but WORKER_API_KEY or
# APP_BASE_URL is missing: polling every tick into a 401 is worse than not
# polling, because it is noise that hides the misconfiguration causing it.
# RESEARCH_QUEUE_POLLER=1
#
# Where the queue lives — the app that serves /api/admin/research/requests/claim.
# APP_BASE_URL=https://app.example.com
#
# WORKER_API_KEY is above: the same key the claim/report endpoints check. The
# worker authenticates as a machine because a user session would expire under it.

# ── Open-web research — NOT A WORKER KEY ────────────────────────────────────
# TAVILY_API_KEY DELIBERATELY DOES NOT BELONG HERE. Removed 2026-08-29, the same
# day it was added.
#
# It was added because worker health warned about it. That warning was itself the
# bug: every consumer of open-web research is an APP module — the four watches,
# lead enrichment, and the CAD-URL guess ("Method 9") in boundary-fetch.service.ts.
# "grep -rn TAVILY worker/src" finds nothing outside that warning, and the deep
# research pipeline has zero open-web references.
#
# So the worker was reading its OWN environment to report on a DIFFERENT process's
# configuration, which it cannot observe. Setting the key here does nothing, and
# documenting it here is precisely what makes somebody do it.
#
# Set TAVILY_API_KEY in Doppler for the APP.

# ── Receipt extraction poller ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Same shape as RESEARCH_QUEUE_POLLER above and off for the same reason: a loop
# that spends Anthropic tokens with no human in it is a deployment decision, not
# a code change. Refuses to start when set without ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, because a
# tick that throws every time is noise hiding its own cause.
# RECEIPT_EXTRACTION_POLLER=1
